# =============================================================================
#  Caddy on .87 -- TLS on the LAN (docs/05 section 5.7)
# =============================================================================
#  Everything user-facing goes through one front door with a certificate that
#  validates, so we never train the team to click through browser warnings.
#
#  `tls internal` issues certificates from a CA Caddy generates locally: no
#  public DNS, no ACME, no egress. That is not a preference here -- Caddy runs on
#  the `internal: true` network (docs/16) and could not reach an ACME server if
#  it wanted to. The cost is that each client machine trusts that CA root once:
#
#      docker compose exec caddy \
#        cat /data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt > caddy-root.crt
#      # Windows: import to "Trusted Root Certification Authorities" (Local Machine)
#      # Linux:   /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/ then update-ca-certificates
#
#  That root lives in the `caddy_data` volume. `docker compose down -v` destroys
#  it and every client has to trust a new one, so copy it off before a level-3
#  removal (../README.md).
#
#  NAME RESOLUTION: add A records for *.ai.lan on the internal DNS server, all
#  pointing at .87. If you do not control DNS, five hosts-file lines per client
#  machine. DNS is worth asking for; hosts files rot.
#
#  Until either exists, the chat UI also answers on the host's bare ADDRESS via
#  $PLATFORM_HOST, so nobody is blocked on a DNS ticket to try the thing. A site
#  block only answers for names it is addressed by: visiting the IP with no such
#  block matches nothing, Caddy has no certificate to offer, and the browser
#  reports ERR_SSL_PROTOCOL_ERROR -- which reads as a broken server rather than
#  as an unconfigured hostname. That is how M1's acceptance test first failed.
#
#  Upstreams are addressed by SERVICE NAME over the internal Docker network
#  (docs/16 section 4.2). There is no public DNS on that network, and there is
#  nothing here that needs any.
# =============================================================================

{
	email ops@internal.invalid

	# A client connecting to a bare IP sends NO SNI -- TLS server names are DNS
	# names, and browsers and curl both omit the extension for IP literals.
	# Caddy selects a certificate by SNI, so with nothing to match it aborts the
	# handshake with an internal-error alert. Having a certificate for the IP is
	# not enough; there has to be a name to look it up by.
	#
	# The browser reports this as ERR_SSL_PROTOCOL_ERROR and curl as
	# "tlsv1 alert internal error", neither of which points at SNI. It reproduces
	# on the host's own loopback, which is what separates it from a firewall.
	default_sni {$PLATFORM_HOST:chat.ai.lan}

	# docs/05 says `admin off`. We bind it to loopback INSIDE the container
	# instead, purely so compose.yaml's healthcheck has something real to ask.
	# 127.0.0.1 here is the container's own loopback: not published, not on any
	# Docker network, unreachable from the LAN and from every other container.
	admin 127.0.0.1:2019
}

# --- Chat UI -- what the team actually visits -------------------------------
#  Addressed by BOTH its name and the host's address: the name for when DNS is
#  in place, the address so the first colleague to try it does not need DNS at
#  all. `tls internal` issues for an IP SAN quite happily.
chat.ai.lan, {$PLATFORM_HOST:chat.invalid} {
	tls internal
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy open-webui:8080 {
		# Streaming responses. A default read timeout that expires mid-answer
		# looks exactly like a model failure and is debugged as one.
		flush_interval -1
	}
}

# --- Fleet dashboard -- the toggle from docs/03 section 4.1 -----------------
#  Bookmark this on each machine's desktop. The status line is the entire point:
#  nobody should have to guess whether the VRAM is actually free, and nobody
#  should have to learn a command.
fleet.ai.lan {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy fleet-controller:8090 {
		# The dashboard pushes SSE events; buffering them defeats the "live"
		# part of a live dashboard.
		flush_interval -1
	}
}

# --- Gateway ----------------------------------------------------------------
#  Clients (OpenCode, Cline) may also hit 10.0.0.87:4000 directly on the LAN.
api.ai.lan {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy litellm:4000 {
		flush_interval -1
	}
	# deep-slow answers can take minutes by design (docs/06: timeout 1800). A
	# reverse-proxy timeout shorter than the model's is a 504 that looks like a
	# gateway bug -- docs/07 section 17, test 3 checks the stream survives Caddy.
	# Raise the transport timeouts here if that test fails; do NOT lower
	# LiteLLM's to match Caddy's.
}

# --- Local image registry (docs/05 section 5.6) -----------------------------
#  The preferred option: TLS, one hostname, no per-host daemon.json drift.
#  compose.yaml also publishes :5000 plain-HTTP so M1 is not blocked on
#  distributing the CA root. When every host trusts the root, delete that
#  publish and the "insecure-registries" line from each host's daemon.json.
registry.ai.lan {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy registry:5000
	# Image layers are large; do not let a body-size default truncate a push.
	request_body {
		max_size 0
	}
}

# --- Document management (RAGFlow, ADR-0007) --------------------------------
#  Two UIs is the acknowledged UX wart of adopting RAGFlow: chat in Open WebUI,
#  documents here. Giving it a real name and a real certificate is the cheapest
#  part of explaining that to users.
docs.ai.lan {
	tls internal
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy ragflow:9380
}
